The thoracic cage is symmetrical on both sides, with the mediastinum and trachea centrally located. There is increased lung markings in both lungs. A soft tissue density mass is observed at the right hilum, measuring approximately 50x38 mm, with lobulated margins and indistinct borders. Surrounding the mass, there are fine spiculations, and the bronchial lumen within is compressed and narrowed, with interruption of its course. Multiple nodules are present in both lungs, some of which are ground-glass opacities and some are solid nodules, with blurred margins and multiple fibrous strands around them. The largest nodule measures approximately 20x16 mm. There are multiple round, well-defined lucent areas without markings in both lungs. Multiple lymph nodes are seen in the mediastinum, some of which are slightly enlarged, with the largest having a short axis of about 1.0 cm. There is localized pleural thickening on both sides. Calcifications are noted in the aorta and coronary artery regions. High-density areas of varying degrees are observed in the left 2nd anterior rib, left 9th posterior rib, right scapula, right 10th costovertebral joint, and L1 vertebral body. Additional findings: The thyroid gland shows heterogeneous density.

Chest HRCT plain scan indicates:
1. A mass at the right hilum with localized narrowing of the right upper lobe bronchus, accompanied by multiple pulmonary nodules, some with ground-glass density, consistent with lung cancer with multiple pulmonary metastases;
2. Slightly enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes; localized pleural thickening on both sides; emphysema and localized bullae formation in both lungs;
3. Atherosclerosis;
4. High likelihood of multiple bone metastases in the left 2nd anterior rib, left 9th posterior rib, right scapula, right 10th costovertebral joint, and L1 vertebral body.

Additional findings: Thyroid changes, recommend correlation with ultrasound.

The lumbar spine exhibits a slight straightening of the normal lordotic curve. Osteophyte formation is observed along the margins of the vertebral bodies, with posterior bony overgrowth at the superior margin of the L2 vertebral body, causing mild compression of the dural sac. There are indentations noted at the superior and inferior margins of the T12 vertebral body and the superior margin of the L2 vertebral body. Disc bulges are present at L1/L2 and L3/4, while disc protrusions are noted at L4/5 and L5/S1, with mild narrowing of the spinal canal at the L1-2 level. No abnormalities are seen in the nerve roots, and there is no hypertrophy or calcification of the ligamentum flavum. A small step-like change is observed at the posterior margin of the L1/L2 vertebral body. No abnormal densities are detected within the spinal canal. There is punctate calcification of the interspinous ligament. Spina bifida occulta is noted at S1.

Lumbar CT scan with axial and 2D reconstruction shows:
1. Disc protrusions at L4/5 and L5/S1; disc bulges at L1/L2 and L3/4 with mild narrowing of the spinal canal at the L1-2 level;
2. Schmorl's nodes formation at the superior and inferior margins of the T12 vertebral body and the superior margin of the L2 vertebral body;
3. Instability at the L1/L2 vertebral body;
4. Degenerative changes in the lumbar spine; calcification of the interspinous ligament; spina bifida occulta at S1.

Please correlate with clinical findings and follow up as necessary.

The demarcation between the gray and white matter of the cerebral hemispheres is relatively clear. Multiple punctate low-density areas are observed in the bilateral basal ganglia and corona radiata, some with well-defined borders. There is decreased density in the periventricular white matter. The ventricular system is enlarged, with widened sulci and deepened gyri, and the midline structures are centered.

The thoracic cage is symmetrical on both sides, with the mediastinum and trachea in the midline. There is increased lung markings in both lungs. Multiple nodules are seen in the left lung, the largest of which (Se7, Im41-42) is a solid nodule measuring approximately 5×4mm. Multiple fibrous strands are observed within both lungs. Encapsulated pleural effusion is noted in the right lung fissure. No abnormal morphology is seen in the lobar and segmental bronchi of both lungs. Calcified lymph nodes are present at both lung hila. Bilateral pleural cavities show arc-shaped low-density fluid collections, with slight atelectasis of the adjacent lung tissue. An arc-shaped fluid density is seen within the pericardium. Calcification is observed in the aortic wall. Additionally noted: multiple low-density cystic lesions in the liver and thickening of the gallbladder wall.

Chest HRCT and brain CT plain scan indicate:
1. Small nodule in the left lung, multiple fibrous strands in both lungs;
2. Encapsulated pleural effusion in the right lung fissure; bilateral pleural effusion with slight atelectasis of adjacent lung tissue;
3. Pericardial effusion;
4. Calcified lymph nodes at both lung hila; atherosclerosis;
5. Multiple ischemic infarcts in the brain, some chronic; brain atrophy, white matter degeneration;
Additionally noted: multiple liver cysts; possible chronic cholecystitis.

Clinical follow-up is recommended.

The thoracic cage is symmetrical, with the mediastinum and trachea in the midline. The lung markings are clear. Multiple nodules are observed in the upper lobes of both lungs and the right middle lobe, some of which are ground-glass and calcified nodules. The largest nodule (Se5, Im61-64) is a ground-glass nodule, measuring approximately 5×4mm. A few linear slightly high-density shadows are seen in both lungs. There is thickening of the bronchial walls in both lobes and segments, with small lymph nodes visible in the mediastinum and both hila. There is localized thickening of the pleura on both sides, and no obvious destructive abnormalities are seen in the bones of the thoracic cage. Calcifications are noted in the aortic valve, aortic wall, and coronary artery regions.

Incidental findings: Multiple small round low-density lesions are seen in the liver parenchyma, and punctate dense shadows are observed in the gallbladder.

