The gray-white matter differentiation in both cerebral hemispheres is still clear. A patchy low-density area is observed in the right corona radiata region. There are spotty low-density areas near the lateral ventricles and basal ganglia on both sides. The density of the parenchyma adjacent to the lateral ventricles is reduced, and the ventricular system is widened. The cisterns, sulci, and fissures are widened. An arc-shaped fluid density is visible beneath the cranial plates in the left frontal, parietal, and temporal regions, as well as the right frontal region, with adjacent brain parenchyma compression. The midline structures are centered.

Non-contrast CT of the brain shows:
