translation:
CT angiography of the lower extremities was performed urgently after intranasal contrast administration.
Data: suspicion of ischemia of the left lower limb, history of left femoral-popliteal bypass placement, history of thrombectomy of the left femoral-popliteal bypass.
The study included the abdominal aorta, both iliac axes, and lower extremities up to the level of the ankles.
The abdominal aorta is massively atherosclerotic, with a tortuous course, and in the infrarenal segment, there is a presence of parietal thrombus up to 0.8 cm thick over approximately 3.5 cm.
Both common iliac arteries are patent, massively atherosclerotic.
External iliac arteries are patent, massively atherosclerotic.
Internal iliac arteries are narrow, massively atherosclerotic.
Left lower extremity:
status post left hip joint arthroplasty - artifacts from metallic elements of the prosthesis;
status post femoral-popliteal bypass - occluded bypass;
collateral circulation vessels in the thigh;
popliteal artery occluded;
trifurcation absent;
the remaining fibular artery is visualized via collateral circulation.
Right lower extremity:
occlusion of the superficial femoral artery starting just below its origin - reestablishes at the level of 1/4 of the distal thigh;
popliteal artery patent;
incomplete trifurcation;
posterior tibial artery occluded;
the vessels of the lower leg are only partially contrast-filled, massively atherosclerotic.
Additionally, in the scope of the study:
ascites - perihepatic and interloop fluid;
numerous cystic lesions in the liver;
small nodule in the left adrenal gland;
heterogeneous bone texture in the study area - probably due to massive bone atrophy;
compression fractures of vertebrae Th10, Th12, and L2.
massive edema of the subcutaneous tissues of both lower limbs.

